#!/bin/sh
# script/board-image: build the VisionFive 2 microSD boot payload (milestone 16a).
#
#   script/board-image
#   script/board-image --soak                 # the sustained multicore workload instead of a halt (219)
#   script/board-image --job-mix              # the multi-tasking workload sweep instead of a halt (168)
#   script/board-image --bench                # the microbenchmark suite instead of a halt, one hart (134)
#   script/board-image --bench --extra-features fastpath_pad   # ...and E3's padded fastpath
#   NIFE_FASTPATH_PAD=0 NIFE_FASTPATH_SHIFT=1640 script/board-image --bench --extra-features fastpath_pad
#                                             # ...one image of E3's layout control (134)
#   script/board-image --soak --reboot        # ...and the board cold-reboots every two minutes (249)
#   script/board-image --card /Volumes/NIFE   # and copy the matched set onto an already-formatted card
#   script/board-image --tftp                 # a boot script that fetches over the network first (257)
#   script/board-image --tftp --server 10.0.0.5   # ...from an address you name rather than one read here
#   script/board-image --th1520               # Scaleway's EM-RV1 instead of radon: a FIT source (89)
#   script/board-image --th1520 --soak        # ...and the workload flags above combine with it
#
# Builds the riscv64 release kernel, strips it to the flat Image format U-Boot's `booti` loads
# (the RISC-V Linux Image header sits at offset 0; boot.s emits it), verifies the header magic,
# packs the userspace archive the kernel measures, writes the U-Boot boot script beside them under
# target/board/, and either copies all three onto a card you name or prints the commands that do.
# See notes/visionfive2.md for the boot path, the DTB caveat, and the bench runbook.
#
# **What it will and will not do to a disk** (milestone 217 answered this deliberately). Formatting
# is still yours: `dd` and `diskutil eraseDisk` name a whole device, they destroy everything on it,
# and the person at the bench is the one looking at the device name. Copying three files onto a
# filesystem you have already mounted and named is not that, and treating the two the same had a
# cost: the printed steps used to copy the kernel without the archive and call the archive
# optional, which is how a card ends up carrying a pair from two different builds and the board
# halts with MEASURED BOOT REFUSED. So `--card` copies the set, as a set, and the mismatch stops
# being something a reader can express.
#
# The one thing it deletes, and only this: a `/extlinux/extlinux.conf` on the card that we wrote.
# An extlinux config on this board does not fail, it **hangs** U-Boot before our kernel runs (see
# milestone 218), so leaving a stale one beside a working boot script would quietly undo the fix.
# It is matched by its content before removal, so somebody else's extlinux config is left alone.
#
# Name: provisional. Minted 2026-08-14 by milestone 16a's lane: it builds the payload image a
# board loads, named for the artifact rather than for the act. The family it belongs to has since
# been ratified around it, which is the strongest thing this block can say. calef signed
# `script/board-netboot` on 2026-09-05 and gave the scheme its reason in doing so: every member
# names the thing rather than its wire, so `board-image` names the image it builds,
# `board-console` names the console it gives you, and `board-tftp` was turned down for naming a
# protocol. This script is the member that scheme was read off. no architect has ratified it
# directly.
#
# Exception, on the record: this entry point only partly delegates to `cargo xtask`. It is a
# build-and-copy step with no QEMU, no test harness, and no shared engine code to reuse; a `sh`
# script that calls cargo and llvm-objcopy directly was judged cheaper than an xtask subcommand.
# The line has since moved once, in the direction the original note predicted: the boot script's
# legacy U-Boot header is two CRCs over a 64-byte layout, which is logic with a right answer and a
# wrong one, so it lives in `cargo xtask board-script` where it has tests, and this script calls
# it. Anything else that grows a right answer belongs there for the same reason.
#
# BUGS
#
# - **The kernel is release and the userspace archive is debug.** `cargo xtask
# initial-ramdisk-riscv` packs
#   target/<target>/debug/ because nothing on this path sets xtask's release flag, so every board
#   payload, and every job-mix and soak number taken from one, runs debug programs under a release
#   kernel. On radon (2026-10-05) the syscall stub alone was 3 to 8 ticks of a 110-tick null-syscall
#   job (notes/job-mix/radon-2026-10-05.md). Fixing it moves every radon baseline at once, so it is
#   a proposal with a re-baseline, not a flag flip:
#   design/roadmap/0797-release-userspace-in-board-images.md.

set -eu
# A heavy job: wait for one of the machine's heavy-job slots first (helpers/heavy_slot.py).
. "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/heavy-slot.sh"
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# This project's own QEMU, if `script/ci-qemu` has built one (helpers/qemu-path.sh says why
# PATH is the mechanism). A no-op on macOS and on any machine without that prefix.
. helpers/qemu-path.sh

# **The soak payload** (milestone 219): the same image, with the kernel feature that replaces the
# halt at the end of the boot tour with a pool of user-mode IPC workers and a heartbeat. It is a
# flag here rather than a second script because everything else about the payload is identical, and
# because the archive/kernel ORDER below is the thing that must not be re-derived by hand: a lane
# that built the soak kernel separately would rebuild it against the previous archive and meet
# MEASURED BOOT REFUSED at the bench, which is boot 12 all over again. See notes/soak.md.
# **The rebooting payload** (milestone 249): the soak, plus a cold reboot every two minutes, so
# the boot-time thread-placement lottery can be drawn fifty times unattended instead of nine times
# by a person walking to the board. It is a second flag rather than a second value of --soak
# because it is a different promise about what the card will do to the machine, and the operator
# should have to type the word "reboot" to get it. The build prints what it is and how to stop it
# before it does anything, below.
# **The benchmark payload** (milestone 134's E1, E3 and E4 on radon): the same image, with the
# `bench` feature that runs the microbenchmark suite instead of the tour, and with `single_hart`,
# which is not optional here. E1 and E4 require ONE hart so their threads contend for the same
# core's cache; QEMU gets that from `-smp 1` on a runner's command line and a card has no command
# line, so a `board,bench` card would boot four U74s and both benchmarks would self-skip on the
# exact machine they were designed for. The two features go on together for that reason, rather
# than being offered separately for somebody to get wrong at the bench. The cost is stated where a
# reader meets it, in `single_hart`'s own entry in kernel/Cargo.toml: `smp_throughput`, `fs_read`
# and `fs_throughput` skip on this card. See notes/footprint-perturbation.md.
FEATURES=board
TH1520=no
SOAK=no
JOB_MIX=no
REBOOT=no
BENCH=no
EXTRA=""
CARD=""
# **The network-first boot script** (milestone 257). Off by default and it stays off by default: a
# script that fetches over the network is a promise about a machine that has to be running, so it is
# asked for rather than arrived at. The card path is unchanged whether this is set or not, because
# the network script keeps the card underneath it as its fallback. See `cargo xtask board-script`.
TFTP=no
SERVER=""
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
        --soak) SOAK=yes; shift ;;
        --job-mix) JOB_MIX=yes; shift ;;
        --reboot) REBOOT=yes; shift ;;
        --bench) BENCH=yes; shift ;;
        --extra-features)
            [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "board-image: --extra-features needs a comma-separated list" >&2; exit 2; }
            EXTRA="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --card)
            [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "board-image: --card needs a path" >&2; exit 2; }
            CARD="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --tftp) TFTP=yes; shift ;;
        --th1520) TH1520=yes; shift ;;
        --server)
            [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "board-image: --server needs an address" >&2; exit 2; }
            SERVER="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        *) echo "board-image: unknown argument $1 (--soak, --job-mix, --reboot, --bench, --th1520, --extra-features <list>, --card <dir>, --tftp, --server <ip>)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
done

# --reboot is only meaningful over a soak, and the kernel feature says the same thing in its own
# `[features]` entry. Refusing here rather than quietly implying --soak, because the two flags
# together are what the notes and the roadmap both spell, and a card built from a command nobody
# has written down is a card nobody can reproduce.
if [ -n "$SERVER" ] && [ "$TFTP" = no ]; then
    echo "board-image: --server only means something with --tftp" >&2
    exit 2
fi
if [ "$REBOOT" = yes ] && [ "$SOAK" = no ]; then
    echo "board-image: --reboot needs --soak (there is nothing to reboot between)" >&2
    exit 2
fi
# **The multi-tasking workload payload** (milestone 168): the same image, with the kernel feature
# that replaces the halt at the end of the boot tour with a sweep of EL0 tasks through a
# heterogeneous job mix, reported as jobs per minute against task count. It is the instrument
# design/decisions/0096-process-kernel-or-event-kernel.md is waiting on, and its gate is HARDWARE, so
# a card is how the number is actually taken. A flag here rather than a second script for --soak's
# reason: everything else about the payload is identical, and the archive/kernel ORDER below is the
# thing that must not be re-derived by hand. See notes/job-mix.md.
# `--bench` replaces the end of the boot tour too, so it is exclusive with the other two for the
# same reason and with the same refusal rather than a silent preference.
if [ "$BENCH" = yes ] && { [ "$SOAK" = yes ] || [ "$JOB_MIX" = yes ]; }; then
    echo "board-image: --bench, --soak and --job-mix all replace the end of the boot tour; pick one" >&2
    exit 2
fi
if [ "$SOAK" = yes ] && [ "$JOB_MIX" = yes ]; then
    # Both features replace the end of the boot tour, and the kernel would run the sweep and never
    # soak. Refused here rather than silently preferring one, because a card built from an ambiguous
    # command is a card nobody can reproduce.
    echo "board-image: --soak and --job-mix both replace the end of the boot tour; pick one" >&2
    exit 2
fi
if [ "$SOAK" = yes ]; then FEATURES="board,soak_test"; fi
if [ "$JOB_MIX" = yes ]; then FEATURES="board,job_mix"; fi
if [ "$BENCH" = yes ]; then FEATURES="board,bench,single_hart"; fi
if [ "$REBOOT" = yes ]; then
    FEATURES="board,soak_test,reboot_soak_test"
    cat >&2 <<'WARNING'

board-image: --reboot builds a card that makes the board RESET ITSELF every two minutes,
             forever, and draw the placement lottery again each time. Before you leave it:

  * Press a key on the serial console on the FIRST boot and confirm the kernel answers
    `soak-test-reboot: DISARMED`. That is the only proof the escape works on your cable, and
    nothing in the kernel can prove it for you: a UART cannot receive a byte it sends.
  * The escape is any byte, checked every 5s and again 5s before each reset.
  * If the console is lost entirely, U-Boot's autoboot countdown on the next boot is the
    fallback, and rewriting the card is the one after that.

  See notes/soak.md, "The rebooting soak on radon, which is milestone 249's experiment".

WARNING
fi

# **The extra features, appended last** (milestone 134's E3). `fastpath_pad` is the one this exists
# for: E3 compares a padded IPC fastpath against an un-padded one on the SAME machine, so it needs
# two cards that differ in exactly one Cargo feature and in nothing else. Appending rather than
# replacing is what keeps that true.
#
# **The hazard this cannot fix, so it prints instead.** Both cards write the same three filenames
# into target/board/, so a padded and an un-padded image are indistinguishable once they are on a
# card, and a bench session that mixes them up produces a comparison of a build against itself. The
# feature set is echoed below and it is the one line worth copying into the log beside the numbers.
# **The second RISC-V machine** (milestone 89 (Scaleway EM-RV1)): the same payload for the T-Head
# TH1520 in Scaleway's Elastic Metal RV1. `board_th1520` implies `board`, so it takes `board`'s place
# at the head of whichever list the flags above chose, and every workload flag still means what it
# means on radon. The RV1 has no card and no network boot of ours: it boots a FIT image from its
# own eMMC, so --card and --tftp do not apply.
if [ "$TH1520" = yes ]; then
    if [ -n "$CARD" ] || [ "$TFTP" = yes ]; then
        echo "board-image: --th1520 builds a FIT source for the RV1's eMMC; --card and --tftp are radon's" >&2
        exit 2
    fi
    FEATURES="board_th1520${FEATURES#board}"
fi

if [ -n "$EXTRA" ]; then FEATURES="$FEATURES,$EXTRA"; fi

# **E3's layout control reads two variables, and only with `fastpath_pad`** (milestone 134,
# kernel/build.rs). Set without the feature they would be silently ignored and the operator would
# boot a kernel that is not the image they asked for, so that is refused. With it, their values join
# the `features:` line below, because nothing else here records which E3 image was built.
E3=""
if [ -n "${NIFE_FASTPATH_PAD:-}${NIFE_FASTPATH_SHIFT:-}" ]; then
    case ",$FEATURES," in
        *,fastpath_pad,*) ;;
        *) echo "board-image: NIFE_FASTPATH_PAD/NIFE_FASTPATH_SHIFT are set but fastpath_pad is not in the features; add --extra-features fastpath_pad or unset them" >&2
           exit 2 ;;
    esac
fi
case ",$FEATURES," in
    *,fastpath_pad,*) E3=" (NIFE_FASTPATH_PAD=${NIFE_FASTPATH_PAD:-1} NIFE_FASTPATH_SHIFT=${NIFE_FASTPATH_SHIFT:-0})" ;;
esac

# Checked before the build rather than after it, so a typo costs a message instead of four minutes
# of cargo. The refusals are narrow on purpose: this writes into a directory somebody named, and
# the only paths it declines are the ones where "somebody named it" is not plausible.
if [ -n "$CARD" ]; then
    if [ ! -d "$CARD" ]; then
        echo "board-image: --card $CARD is not a directory (mount the card first)" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    case "$CARD" in
        /) echo "board-image: refusing to write the payload to /" >&2; exit 1 ;;
    esac
fi

TARGET=riscv64imac-unknown-none-elf
ELF="target/$TARGET/release/kernel"
OUT="target/board"
IMG="$OUT/nife-vf2.img"
if [ "$TH1520" = yes ]; then IMG="$OUT/nife-th1520.img"; fi

# The userspace archive FIRST, then the kernel, and the order is load-bearing: packing the
# archive regenerates target/init-measure-riscv.txt, the manifest the kernel compiles in as its
# measured-boot trust root. Kernel-first built a kernel vouching for the PREVIOUS archive, and on
# 2026-08-15 the VisionFive 2 refused the pair at the bench (MEASURED BOOT REFUSED, boot 12): the
# gate working, aimed at our own build script. QEMU never hit it because xtask orders these
# correctly; this script did not.
cargo xtask initial-ramdisk-riscv
mkdir -p "$OUT"
cp target/initial-ramdisk-riscv.img "$OUT/nife-initial-ramdisk.img"

cargo build -p kernel --release --target "$TARGET" --features "$FEATURES"

# llvm-objcopy from the pinned toolchain's llvm-tools component, found the same way xtask finds it
# (rustc --print sysroot). The Homebrew/system objcopy is deliberately not a fallback: a silent
# tool substitution is how a subtly different image gets flashed.
SYSROOT="$(rustc --print sysroot)"
HOST="$(rustc -vV | sed -n 's/^host: //p')"
OBJCOPY="$SYSROOT/lib/rustlib/$HOST/bin/llvm-objcopy"
if [ ! -x "$OBJCOPY" ]; then
    echo "board-image: no llvm-objcopy at $OBJCOPY" >&2
    echo "board-image: the llvm-tools rustup component provides it (see rust-toolchain.toml)" >&2
    exit 1
fi

"$OBJCOPY" -O binary "$ELF" "$IMG"

# The one field U-Boot checks before agreeing to boot this file: the u32 magic 0x05435352
# ("RSC\x05") at offset 0x38 (u-boot arch/riscv/lib/image.c). Catch a headerless or truncated
# image here, at build time, rather than as `Bad Linux RISCV Image magic!` at the bench.
MAGIC="$(od -An -t x4 -j 56 -N 4 "$IMG" | tr -d ' \n')"
if [ "$MAGIC" != "05435352" ]; then
    echo "board-image: $IMG has no RISC-V Image header magic at offset 0x38 (got '$MAGIC')" >&2
    exit 1
fi

# The RV1's payload stops here: a FIT source naming the kernel and the archive, which `mkimage`
# turns into `boot.itb` on the RV1 itself (a stock macOS host has no `mkimage`; the RV1's Debian does).
# The device tree and OpenSBI come out of Scaleway's own `boot.itb` on that machine, so this tree
# guesses neither. The runbook is milestone 89's block, "Rented, hour one".
if [ "$TH1520" = yes ]; then
    ITS="$OUT/nife-th1520.its"
    ENV="$OUT/nife-th1520-env.txt"
    # Scaleway's U-Boot reads these two keys out of the FIT's `env` image (their em-rv1 repo's
    # fit/em-rv1-env.txt). nife reads no command line, so the bootargs are empty on purpose.
    printf 'usr_bootargs=\nusr_has_ramdisk=1\n' > "$ENV"
    cat > "$ITS" <<'ITS'
/dts-v1/;
/*
 * nife on the T-Head TH1520 (Scaleway EM-RV1), milestone 89. Written by script/board-image --th1520.
 *
 * Modeled on Scaleway's fit/em-rv1-c4m16s128-a-boot.its (github.com/scaleway/em-rv1), with three
 * changes. The kernel loads at 0x8020_0000, where nife is linked, rather than Linux's 0x20_0000, and
 * entry names the same address in case this U-Boot reads it. The archive rides as a ramdisk, so
 * U-Boot writes /chosen/linux,initrd-start for the kernel to find. And `fdt` and `opensbi` are the
 * files extracted from the machine's own boot.itb, so the firmware tree and the firmware are
 * Scaleway's rather than this tree's guess.
 */
/ {
    description = "nife on the TH1520, milestone 89";
    #address-cells = <2>;
    #size-cells = <0>;
    images {
        kernel {
            description = "nife";
            data = /incbin/("nife-th1520.img");
            type = "kernel";
            arch = "riscv";
            os = "linux";
            compression = "none";
            load = <0x0 0x80200000>;
            entry = <0x0 0x80200000>;
            hash {
                algo = "sha256";
            };
        };
        ramdisk {
            description = "nife userspace archive, measured by the kernel";
            data = /incbin/("nife-initial-ramdisk.img");
            type = "ramdisk";
            arch = "riscv";
            os = "linux";
            compression = "none";
            load = <0x0 0x90000000>;
            hash {
                algo = "sha256";
            };
        };
        opensbi {
            description = "OpenSBI, Scaleway's, extracted from boot.itb.scaleway";
            data = /incbin/("fw_dynamic.bin");
            type = "opensbi";
            arch = "riscv";
            compression = "none";
            load = <0x0 0x0000000>;
            hash {
                algo = "sha256";
            };
        };
        fdt {
            description = "device tree, Scaleway's, extracted from boot.itb.scaleway";
            data = /incbin/("rv1.dtb");
            type = "flat_dt";
            arch = "riscv";
            compression = "none";
            load = <0x0 0x02800000>;
            hash {
                algo = "sha256";
            };
        };
        env {
            description = "User environment file.";
            data = /incbin/("nife-th1520-env.txt");
            compression = "none";
            hash {
                algo = "sha256";
            };
        };
    };
    configurations {};
};
ITS
    SIZE="$(wc -c < "$IMG" | tr -d ' ')"
    echo
    echo "built, for the TH1520 (Scaleway EM-RV1):"
    echo "  features: $FEATURES$E3"
    echo "  $IMG  ($SIZE bytes, Image header verified)"
    echo "  $OUT/nife-initial-ramdisk.img  (the userspace archive this kernel measures; one pair with it)"
    echo "  $ITS"
    echo "  $ENV"
    echo
    echo "Copy all four to the RV1 and build boot.itb there (milestone 89's block is the runbook):"
    echo "  scp $IMG $OUT/nife-initial-ramdisk.img $ITS $ENV <user>@<rv1-ip>:nife/"
    exit 0
fi

# The card used to carry an `extlinux.conf`, and it could never have worked (milestone 218). With
# no `fdt` line in the label U-Boot hands `bootm` no device tree at all, and RISC-V refuses rather
# than guessing: `Device tree not found or missing FDT support`, then `### ERROR ### Please RESET
# the board ###`, which is a hang, before a single instruction of ours runs. What goes on the card
# instead is a U-Boot script carrying the exact commands the 2026-09-01 bench boot proves, so the
# boot sequence is unchanged and only the typing is gone. See notes/visionfive2.md.
if [ "$TFTP" = yes ]; then
    # The network-first script (257). The address is not a constant anywhere in this tree: xtask
    # reads it off this machine's own interfaces, or takes the one named here, and the board echoes
    # it at boot so a console log says what the card expects. See notes/visionfive2.md.
    if [ -n "$SERVER" ]; then
        cargo xtask board-script --tftp --server "$SERVER"
    else
        cargo xtask board-script --tftp
    fi
else
    cargo xtask board-script
fi

SIZE="$(wc -c < "$IMG" | tr -d ' ')"
ARCHIVE_SIZE="$(wc -c < "$OUT/nife-initial-ramdisk.img" | tr -d ' ')"
echo
echo "built:"
echo "  features: $FEATURES$E3"
echo "  $IMG  ($SIZE bytes, Image header verified)"
echo "  $OUT/nife-initial-ramdisk.img  ($ARCHIVE_SIZE bytes, the userspace archive this kernel measures)"
if [ "$TFTP" = yes ]; then
    echo "  boot.scr.uimg fetches over the network first and falls back to the card (257)"
    echo "  serve them:  script/board-netboot"
fi
echo
echo "The kernel and the archive are ONE pair. The kernel compiles in a hash of the archive it was"
echo "built against and halts rather than hand the progenitor an archive that does not match, so a card that"
echo "carries a new kernel over an old archive stops at MEASURED BOOT REFUSED. Copy all three files"
echo "or none, which is what --card is for."
echo

# **Say it out loud rather than only claiming it**, which is milestone 223 (read a card and say whether its kernel and archive match). The ORDER above is what makes
# the pair match, and an order is rung two at best: it held on 2026-08-15 and the board refused the
# pair anyway, because the kernel had been built by something other than this script. So the payload
# is checked before it is copied anywhere, which costs a fraction of a second against a four-minute
# build and turns "these were built in the right order" from a claim in a comment into a fact this
# run has read off the bytes. It is the board's own question, asked here.
cargo xtask card-check "$OUT"
echo
# The copy, when a card was named. It happens here, after every build step has succeeded, so a
# failed build cannot leave a card holding half a pair.
if [ -n "$CARD" ]; then
    # A stale nife extlinux config hangs this board (milestone 218). Matched by content first: an
    # extlinux.conf that is not one of ours belongs to whoever put it there.
    STALE="$CARD/extlinux/extlinux.conf"
    if [ -f "$STALE" ] && grep -q '^label nife$' "$STALE"; then
        rm "$STALE"
        rmdir "$CARD/extlinux" 2>/dev/null || true
        echo "removed $STALE"
        echo "  a nife extlinux config on this board hangs U-Boot before the kernel runs (218)."
    fi

    cp "$IMG" "$CARD/nife-vf2.img"
    cp "$OUT/nife-initial-ramdisk.img" "$CARD/nife-initial-ramdisk.img"
    cp "$OUT/boot.scr.uimg" "$CARD/boot.scr.uimg"
    sync

    echo "copied onto $CARD, as one set:"
    echo "  nife-vf2.img, nife-initial-ramdisk.img, boot.scr.uimg"
    echo
    # And again against the card itself (milestone 223), which is a different claim from the one
    # above: this reads what actually landed, so a copy that ran out of space or wrote onto a card
    # somebody else's payload was already on is caught here rather than at the bench.
    cargo xtask card-check "$CARD"
    echo
    echo "Eject before pulling the card, then power the board; it needs nothing typed at it:"
    echo "  diskutil eject \"$CARD\"    # macOS; umount elsewhere"
    echo "  script/board-console --until banner --for 120s --log target/board/boot.log"
    echo
    exit 0
fi

cat <<'EOF'
To prepare a microSD card (macOS). Steps 1 and 2 are yours: they name a whole device and destroy
everything on it. Step 3 is this script's, so the kernel and the archive cannot be copied apart.

  1. Find the card, and be certain of the name; dd to the wrong disk destroys it:
       diskutil list
  2. One FAT32 partition is all U-Boot needs (this erases the card):
       diskutil eraseDisk FAT32 NIFE MBR /dev/diskN
  3. Copy the matched set, and eject:
       script/board-image --card /Volumes/NIFE
       diskutil eject /dev/diskN

If the card came from somewhere else, or was written by hand, or has been sitting in a drawer,
ask whether its kernel and archive are still one pair BEFORE you walk to the board. It reads the
card and writes nothing; 0 means it boots, 1 means it would halt at MEASURED BOOT REFUSED:

     script/card-check /Volumes/NIFE

Then power the board with a terminal already attached. Nothing has to be typed at U-Boot: the
boot script on the card runs the sequence below by itself.

  script/board-console --until banner --for 120s --log target/board/boot.log

If the board reaches the `StarFive #` prompt instead, the script did not run, and the same
commands typed by hand are still the fallback (this is what boot.scr.uimg contains):

  StarFive # load mmc 1:1 ${kernel_addr_r} /nife-vf2.img
  StarFive # load mmc 1:1 0x90000000 /nife-initial-ramdisk.img
  StarFive # setenv nife_archive_size ${filesize}
  StarFive # fdt addr ${fdtcontroladdr}
  StarFive # fdt move ${fdtcontroladdr} 0x86000000
  StarFive # booti ${kernel_addr_r} 0x90000000:${nife_archive_size} 0x86000000

...and the two things to read out while you are there, because they are what says why the script
did not run: `printenv scriptaddr` and `printenv fdt_addr_r`.

EOF

if [ "$TFTP" = yes ]; then
cat <<'EOF'
This card's boot script tries the network FIRST and falls back to these same files on the card,
so write it once and leave it in the board. On the serving machine, in another terminal:

  script/board-netboot                # serves target/board on udp/69, no sudo needed

The board announces which address it will ask, before it asks:

  nife: tftp server is 192.168.8.x, setenv nife_boot_server to point somewhere else

If that is not this machine's address any more (a lease moved), the fix is one line at the
U-Boot prompt and no card at all:

  StarFive # setenv nife_boot_server <address>
  StarFive # source ${scriptaddr}

...and rebuilding the card's script with `script/board-image --tftp --card /Volumes/NIFE` is
what makes it stick.
EOF
fi
